\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Capability profile on the common covered set} (\leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$n{=}1{,}000$}). Columns correspond to the unaligned policy, the answer-only control, the candidate-aligned policy, and the final \textsc  {EGO}{} model, respectively. Continuations form \leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$31.0\%$} of the set (\leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$310/1{,}000$}); their stable precision shows that the main limitation is recovery, not noisier continuation guesses. Evidence utility is the SelAcc difference between traces that do and do not explicitly invoke a prior-action pattern; it is observational, not a causal effect. Because the world-model pool covers \leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$43.4\%$} of held-out anticipation points, the full-set equivalent of \leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$28.8\%$} is \leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$12.5\%$}, against \leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$10.5\%$} for the WM Top-\leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$1$} policy. The final \textsc  {EGO}{} checkpoint is measured in the same cohort; its SelAcc uses the strict denominator, while the conditional capability axes exclude malformed traces following the definitions above.\relax }}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:capability}{{1}{3}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Surface and interventional diagnostics.} Evidence utility is a conditional association and is interpreted descriptively. Belief-swap sensitivity is interventional: only the belief prefix is swapped while reasoning is held fixed, and the paraphrase-control flip rate is subtracted (\leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$n{=}400$} for Base, GT-only, and Cand.-CE). No belief-swap evaluation was run for the final \textsc  {EGO}{} model, so its entry is left blank rather than transferred from its predecessor. The low echo rate under candidate alignment shows that its belief field is not merely a copy of the selected action.\relax }}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:mechanism}{{2}{3}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Limitation of the final-stage intervention.}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Excluded proxy.}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {A state-bearing belief enables correction when an action-copying belief does not.} The four evaluated policies receive the same observation, completed-action history, shuffled candidate set, and prompt. Generated traces are reproduced verbatim, with \ldots  {} marking omitted text. To avoid anecdotal selection, we apply a fixed criterion to the common covered set: all four traces must be valid, GT-only must copy its chosen action into the belief field and answer incorrectly, and \textsc  {EGO}{} must answer correctly. This criterion identifies \leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$31$} of \leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$915$} points; the example shown is one of the \leavevmode {\color  {measuredblue}$18$} for which Cand.-CE is also incorrect. \underline  {Underlined} spans explicitly reference prior actions under the same rule used for the mention-rate analysis, whereas \textbf  {bold} spans express the model's current task state.\relax }}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
